<title>UK: Bonny crude to face delays, not cancellations-Shell.</title>
<headline>Bonny crude to face delays, not cancellations-Shell.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-04-22</dateline>
<text>
<p>Some Nigerian Bonny crude oil exports are likely to be delayed but cancellations are not expected due to the force majuere declared on Monday by Shell Nigeria, said a Shell spokesman in London.</p>
<p>&quot;The length of delay will be determined by how things develop over the next 24 hours,&quot; he said.  </p>
<p>Royal Dutch Shell Group's Nigerian unit said earlier on Tuesday that it had declared force majeure on export of Bonny crude oil because of a strike by contractors that handle the berthing and loading of tankers.</p>
<p>&quot;We have declared force majeure on Bonny exports with effect from yesterday,&quot; a Shell spokesman said in Lagos.</p>
<p>&quot;This action was taken in response to a strike by workers of a contractor that operates support services for tankers berthing and loading at Bonny terminal.&quot;</p>
<p>Shell's Bonny output is about half of the roughly 950,000 barrels per day (bpd) it produces in Nigeria.</p>
<p>The spokesman said the force majeure would hold for as long as the strike by the staff of the oil services company known as O.I.L. lasted.</p>
<p>--London newsroom, +44 171 542 4984</p>
</text>
